            "Postpartum haemorrhage is the leading single cause of maternal death in Ghana and worldwide; a woman can lose her whole circulating volume in minutes from a uterus that will not contract, and most of those deaths happen in the first two hours after birth, at home or in a facility where the response was slow, the drug was missing or the referral took too long. <strong>Primary PPH</strong> is blood loss of <strong>500 mL or more within 24 hours of birth</strong> (1,000 mL after caesarean section), or <strong>any loss that makes the woman unwell</strong> — a small anaemic woman is in trouble at 300 mL; <strong>severe PPH</strong> is 1,000 mL or more. <strong>Secondary PPH</strong> is abnormal bleeding from 24 hours to 12 weeks after birth, usually from retained tissue and infection. The uterus at term receives about 500–700 mL of blood a minute; after the placenta separates, the interlacing myometrial fibres must contract and retract to clamp the spiral arteries — the 'living ligatures' — and anything that stops them (an exhausted, overdistended, infected, full-bladder-displaced or drugged uterus, or tissue left inside) causes <strong>atony</strong>, the cause of about 70% of haemorrhage. The remainder is <strong>trauma</strong> (tears of the perineum, vagina and cervix, episiotomy, uterine rupture, inversion, haematoma — about 20%), <strong>tissue</strong> (retained placenta or fragments, membranes, an abnormally adherent placenta — about 10%) and <strong>thrombin</strong> (coagulopathy from abruption, pre-eclampsia and HELLP, sepsis, amniotic fluid embolism, a dead fetus retained, anticoagulants or inherited disorders — about 1%, but often secondary to massive bleeding from the others).",
